window.__finlexDocLoaded(10221,{"id":10221,"articles":[{"id":1000935,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Establishes the two-tier US GAAP consolidation framework: first evaluate the Variable Interest Entity (VIE) model (primary beneficiary = power + economics), then the Voting Interest Entity (VOE) model (>50% voting shares).","seq":0},{"id":1000936,"no":"ASC 810-10-15","heading":"Dual Consolidation Framework: Step 1 VIE Scope & Criteria","text":"US GAAP requires evaluating consolidation in strict sequence: FIRST the Variable Interest Entity (VIE) model, and ONLY IF not a VIE, the Voting Interest Entity (VOE) model.\n\n[When Is an Entity a VIE? (ASC 810-10-15-14) — Meet ANY One Criterion]\n• (a) Insufficient Equity at Risk: Total equity investment at risk is insufficient to finance activities without subordinated financial support (<10% rule-of-thumb rebuttable presumption).\n• (b) Equity Holders Lack Characteristics of a Controlling Financial Interest: As a group, equity holders at risk lack (i) power to direct the most significant economic activities, (ii) obligation to absorb expected losses, OR (iii) right to receive expected residual returns.\n• (c) Anti-Abuse / Disproportionate Voting Rights: Voting rights of some investors are not proportional to their economics AND substantially all activities are conducted on behalf of an investor with disproportionately few voting rights.\n• Note: Classic China-concept US-listed VIE structures (contractual control via WFOE agreements) are consolidated directly under the VIE model.","seq":1},{"id":1000937,"no":"ASC 810-10-25","heading":"VIE Primary Beneficiary: Power + Economics & Kick-Out Rights","text":"The party that consolidates a VIE is its Primary Beneficiary — the variable interest holder with BOTH (ASC 810-10-25-38A):\n• (1) Power Criterion: The power to direct the activities of the VIE that MOST SIGNIFICANTLY impact the VIE's economic performance (unilateral substantive kick-out rights held by a single unrelated party can negate power).\n• (2) Economics Criterion: The obligation to absorb losses OR the right to receive benefits from the VIE that COULD POTENTIALLY BE SIGNIFICANT to the VIE.\n• Related-Party Tiebreaker (ASC 810-10-25-42~44): When no single party meets both criteria individually, a related-party group under common control evaluates who is 'most closely associated' with the VIE.","seq":2},{"id":1000938,"no":"ASC 810-10-15 / 40","heading":"Step 2 Voting Interest Model (VOE) & Deconsolidation","text":"• Voting Interest Model (ASC 810-10-15-8): For non-VIE corporations, consolidation generally requires >50% of outstanding voting shares, UNLESS noncontrolling shareholders hold substantive participating rights (e.g., veto over budgets/CEO hiring). Unlike IFRS 10, US GAAP has NO 'de facto control' concept for <50% stakes and ignores unexercised potential voting rights.\n• Limited Partnerships (non-VIE): General Partner consolidates unless Limited Partners hold substantive kick-out rights (simple majority) or participating rights.\n• Deconsolidation (ASC 810-10-40-4~5): Upon loss of controlling financial interest in a subsidiary (business), derecognize assets/liabilities/NCI, remeasure any retained noncontrolling investment to FAIR VALUE, and recognize full gain/loss in net income.","seq":3},{"id":1000939,"no":"ASC 810 vs IFRS 10 / CAS 33","heading":"Key Cross-Framework Divergences (Dual VIE/VOE vs. Single Control Model)","text":"• Architecture: ASC 810 uses a rules-heavy TWO-TIER model (VIE then VOE); IFRS 10 / CAS 33 apply a SINGLE principles-based control model (power + variable returns + link).\n• De Facto Control & Potential Voting Rights: IFRS 10 consolidates a <50% holder with de facto control over dispersed shareholders and factors in substantive options/convertibles; ASC 810 VOE strictly requires >50% voting shares.\n• Uniform Accounting Policies & Reporting Dates: IFRS 10 requires uniform accounting policies across the group; ASC 810 does NOT require conforming a subsidiary's valid alternative GAAP policy (e.g., LIFO vs. FIFO). Both allow up to a 3-month lag for subsidiary reporting dates (with significant intervening events disclosed/adjusted).","seq":4},{"id":1000940,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":5}]});
